{"kind":"assert","text":"SECURITY, critical, reproduced by execution: any actor holding the ratifier role can capture a workroom irreversibly, using the sanctioned role-revoke command, in one step.\n\ndecideSupersede authorises a supersession if the actor wrote the target OR holds ratifier — with no floor and no comparison of standing. That includes the genesis operator seed. And decideState's seed branch is positional, keyed on record.index == 0, so once retired it can never be re-run.\n\nMeasured in a fresh workroom. Start: op holds [operator, participant, ratifier], mal holds [participant, ratifier] by an ordinary, legitimate grant. Then a single command — gs role-revoke --as mal --actor op --role operator — and op is not demoted but gone: the roster returns mal only. op has lost operator, ratifier and participant, and no longer appears in Projection.Actors at all.\n\nRecovery from inside the protocol is closed. op self-granting operator fails with \"actor has no live roster membership\". Superseding mal's act fails with \"actor may not supersede target\". Re-seeding is impossible because the seed branch is positional. The founding operator is locked out of their own workroom by one command from an actor they trusted with a routine role — and ratifier is granted routinely, and derived automatically from operator, so the population holding it is not small.\n\nA second finding composes with this one and makes revocation unreliable even before capture. Retirement is a reversible counter and its reversal is authorised by \"did I write it\" rather than \"may I do this now\", while RoleSources is built from active grants only. So a ratifier can self-author and self-ratify a spare grant, supersede it to hide it — at which point gs actors and role_sources are indistinguishable from a clean world — wait for the operator to revoke their visible grant, then un-retire the spare unilaterally and be a ratifier again. Revocation is provably incomplete and the operator gets no signal, because the dormant grant is invisible to every command that would show it.\n\nThese two should be treated as one fix. The shape is that standing is unbounded: ratifier can retire anything, self-conferral is not excluded, and reversal re-checks authorship rather than current authority. Candidate closures: refuse supersession of the index-0 seed; require the superseder's standing to dominate the target's, so a ratifier may not retire an operator's basis; require two distinct ratifiers for governance retirement; forbid self-ratification of one's own roster grant; re-evaluate authority for the class of the target when a supersession is itself superseded; and list retired roster grants in gs actors so a hidden grant is visible.\n\nWorth stating what held, because it is the useful half. Signature authentication and the commitment-identity rules are sound and survived every attack: a non-performer cannot promise, a non-promisor cannot report, and only the requester may ratify a report — a live ratifier is refused there, which is the one place the design is genuinely tight. A grant citing another actor's membership is inert, correctly. Idempotency keys are actor-scoped. Cycles and future citations are structurally impossible. The problem is not authentication; it is that authority, once held, has no ceiling and no durable revocation.\n\nFound by an adversarial review of the authority model; I reproduced the capture myself before recording it. This concerns the substrate on main, not any branch under review."}